H^{1}_0(D)$ solves $\diver(f_0\nabla w)=\diver(e\nabla u_m)$, with
$\operatorname{supp}(e\nabla u_m)\subset\operatorname{supp}e$ at
distance $\gtrsim c_1$ from $\partial D$. The $u$-image of an
\emph{interior} conductivity perturbation gains a full degree by the
same mechanism as Lemma~\ref{lem:scale2}: the source
$F:=e\nabla u_m$ is a vector field oscillating at scale $\delta$ with
mean zero on each bump (the patches $\psi_m$ have $\int\psi_m=0$, so
$\int_D e\nabla u_m\,dx=-\int_D u_m\nabla e\,dx$ and the second
factors are at scale $\gtrsim\delta$; compare the multipole expansion
in the harmonic-correction step), supported away from $\partial D$.
Testing against $\phi=-\Delta^{-1}_{f_0}\psi$ as in
Lemma~\ref{lem:apriori}(iii),
\[
\inner{w}{\psi}
=-\int_D e\nabla u_m\cdot\nabla\phi\,dx ,
\]
and the right side is the pairing of $F$ with $\nabla\phi$: since $F$
is divergence-free to first order on its support (each bump is
mean-zero and $\nabla u_m$ varies at scale $\gtrsim\delta$), only the
\emph{variation} of $\nabla\phi$ across a bump contributes --- one
power of $\delta$ --- while on the boundary $\nabla\phi$ probes $F$
through the Green kernel, whose mean-zero bump data kills the
monopole and leaves the dipole term $O(\delta\norm{F}_2)$; all
monopole/mean contributions vanish exactly as in
Lemma~\ref{lem:scale2}'s Poisson/mean-oscillation estimate. Hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:finebias}
\norm{w}_2=\norm{u_{f_0}-u_m}_2
\ \lesssim\ \delta\,\norm{e\nabla u_m}_2
\ \lesssim\ \delta\,\norm{e}_2
\ \lesssim\ \delta^{1+\alpha}.
\end{equation}
The key point is that $e$ is interior and $m=f_0$ at the boundary, so
\emph{no collar term} $r\delta^\alpha$ survives: the boundary-layer
content that would lose the degree has been absorbed into
$\Pi_\delta$. This bounds every term on the right of \eqref{eq:basic}
at order $\delta^{1+\alpha}$ (which is $\le\delta^{2\alpha}/\delta$
after inversion).
Thus
$\norm Z_2=\norm{P_\delta(u_{f_0}-u_m)}_2
\lesssim\delta^{1+\alpha}+r\delta^\alpha$.
